RT_UPDATED for that cart to get the items. Or simpler yet, just track all CART_UPDATED events and count product occurrences from them.\n\nActually wait - let me re-read the Analytics service requirements: \"rank products by order quantity\". This specifically says \"order quantity\", not \"cart quantity\". So it needs to know what products were in orders.\n\nHmm, but the events don't carry that info directly in OrderCreatedEvent. Let me think about how to handle this...\n\nOption 1: Analytics listens for CART_UPDATED and remembers the items, then when ORDER_CREATED comes with a cartId, it correlates. This is doable.\n\nOption 2: The system architecture expects some other mechanism.\n\nI'll go with option 1 - store cart items when CART_UPDATED fires, and when ORDER_CREATED fires with a cartId, look up the stored items and count them for top products.\n\nFor conversion rate: orders / unique carts. I'll count unique cartIds from CART_UPDATED events and count orders from ORDER_CREATED events.\n\nFor revenue: listen for PAYMENT_PROCESSED with status SUCCESS. But the PaymentProcessedEvent doesn't include the amount. Hmm... So Analytics might need to store order amounts (from ORDER_CREATED events) and look them up when a payment succeeds.\n\nOK wait - I realize there might be an issue. The PaymentProcessedEvent has orderId, status, transactionId? and error?. The amount isn't in the payload. So Analytics needs to correlate ORDER_CREATED events (which have total) with PAYMENT_PROCESSED events (which have orderId).\n\nSo the approach:\n1. Listen for ORDER_CREATED → store orderTotal by orderId\n2. Listen for PAYMENT_PROCESSED with status SUCCESS → add stored total to revenue\n3. Listen for ORDER_CREATED → count orders\n4. Listen for CART_UPDATED → track unique cartIds\n5. Listen for CART_UPDATED → store cart items by cartId\n6. When ORDER_CREATED comes → look up cart items from step 5 and count for top products\n\nThat works! Let me now plan each service's implementation.\n\nLet me think through each service's internal state:\n\n### Catalog Service\n```typescript\n// In-memory store\nproducts: Map<string, { id: string; name: string; price: number; category: string }>\n```\n- createProduct: generate UUID, store, emit PRODUCT_CREATED, return id\n- getProduct: lookup by id\n- listProducts: filter by category if provided\n- searchProducts: case-insensitive match in name/category\n- updateProduct: partial update by id\n- removeProduct: delete by id, return boolean\n\n### Inventory Service\n```typescript\nstock: Map<string, number>\nthresholds: Map<string, number> // default threshold maybe 0\n```\n- setStock: set value, emit INVENTORY_UPDATED, check low stock threshold\n- getStock: return stock or 0\n- reserve: check if >= quantity, decrement, emit INVENTORY_UPDATED, check threshold. Return false if insufficient\n- release: increment stock, emit INVENTORY_UPDATED, check threshold\n- setLowStockThreshold: set threshold for product\n\n### Cart Service\n```typescript\ncarts: Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; items: Map<string, number> }>\n```\n- createCart: generate UUID, create empty cart\n- addItem: consolidate (if product exists, increment quantity)\n- removeItem: delete product from cart\n- getCart: convert items map to array\n- clearCart: remove all items\n- updateQuantity: set specific quantity for product (remove if 0?)\n- Emit CART_UPDATED on any modification\n\n### Pricing Service\n```typescript\nprices: Map<string, number>\ndiscounts: Map<string, number> // code -> percentage\n```\n- On PRODUCT_CREATED: store product price\n- calculateTotal: for each item, get price (from parameter), compute subtotal, apply discount, add 10% tax\n- addDiscountCode: store code and percentage\n- getPrice: return price for product\n\nFor calculateTotal, items already include price, so:\n- subtotal = sum(item.price * item.quantity)\n- if discountCode, discount = subtotal * percentage / 100\n- subtotalAfterDiscount = subtotal - discount\n- tax = subtotalAfterDiscount * 0.10 (10%)\n- total = subtotalAfterDiscount + tax\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Calculate order totals including tax (10% rate). Support registering percentage-based discount codes that reduce the subtotal before tax is applied.\"\n\nSo:\n- subtotal = sum(price * qty)\n- discount = subtotal * (percentage / 100)\n- discounted = subtotal - discount\n- tax = discounted * 0.10\n- total = discounted + tax\n\n### Orders Service\n```typescript\norders: Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number }>\n```\n- createOrder: start PENDING, emit ORDER_CREATED\n- updateStatus: validate transition (PENDING→CONFIRMED/SHIPPED/DELIVERED/CANCELLED?), emit ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED with previous status\n\nStatus transitions allowed:\n- PENDING → CONFIRMED\n- PENDING → CANCELLED\n- CONFIRMED → SHIPPED\n- SHIPPED → DELIVERED\n\nOr more permissive? Let me think about what makes sense:\n- PENDING → any state? Or just CONFIRMED and CANCELLED?\n- Actually, it should follow lifecycle: PENDING → CONFIRMED → SHIPPED → DELIVERED\n- CANCELLED should only be valid from PENDING, or maybe any state?\n\nI'll implement: PENDING → CONFIRMED, PENDING → CANCELLED, CONFIRMED → SHIPPED, SHIPPED → DELIVERED\nIf an invalid transition is attempted, I'll throw an error or just not update.\n\nActually, re-reading the requirements: \"Orders start in a pending state and progress through a status lifecycle (confirmed, shipped, delivered) with the option to cancel.\" So the lifecycle is PENDING → CONFIRMED → SHIPPED → DELIVERED, or PENDING → CANCELLED.\n\nIf an invalid transition is attempted (e.g., CONFIRMED → CANCELLED, or SHIPPED → PENDING), I'll throw an Error.\n\n### Payment Service\n```typescript\noptions?: { mode: 'success' | 'failure' | 'timeout', delayMs?: number }\ntransactions: Map<string, { orderId: string; amount: number; status: string }>\n```\n- processPayment: simulate async payment based on options. Emit PAYMENT_PROCESSED on completion\n- refund: mark transaction as refunded, return success\n\nThe options parameter affects simulation:\n- 'success': payment succeeds after delay\n- 'failure': payment fails after delay\n- 'timeout': payment times out after delay\n\nNeed to generate transactionId on success.\n\n### Notification Service\n```typescript\nsubscriptions: Map<string, string> // userId -> userId (or Set)\norderOwners: Map<string, string> // orderId -> userId\nnotifications: Map<string, Array<...>> // userId -> notifications\n```\n- subscribe: add user to subscription list\n- Listen for ORDER_CREATED: record orderId → userId mapping\n- Listen for ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED: create notification for order's user\n- Listen for PAYMENT_PROCESSED: create notification for order's user\n- getNotifications: return list for user\n\n### Analytics Service\n```typescript\neventCounts: Map<string, number>\nrevenue: number\nuniqueCarts: Set<string>\norderCount: number\ncartItems: Map<string, Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number }>>\nproductOrderCounts: Map<string, number>\n```\n- Listen for all events and count them\n- Listen for PAYMENT_PROCESSED success: add order total to revenue\n- Listen for CART_UPDATED: track unique cartIds, store cart items\n- Listen for ORDER_CREATED: count orders, look up cart items for top products\n\nNow let me implement all these. I'll create a contract first, then implement each service, then test.\n\nActually, given this is a benchmark evaluation, let me focus on getting all 8 services working correctly and passing `npm run build` and `npm run lint`. I'll also write a comprehensive test file.\n\nLet me write everything systematically.\n\nFor the main index.ts, I need to export:\n```typescript\nexport { createCatalogService } from './catalog/index.js';\nexport { createInventoryService } from './inventory/index.js';\nexport { createCartService } from './cart/index.js';\nexport { createPricingService } from './pricing/index.js';\nexport { createOrderService } from './orders/index.js';\nexport { createPaymentService } from './payment/index.js';\nexport { createNotificationService } from './notifications/index.js';\nexport { createAnalyticsService } from './analytics/index.js';\n```\n\nSince the module resolution is Node16, I need `.js` extensions in imports.\n\nLet me start writing each file. I'll go through each service carefully.\n\n### Catalog Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { CatalogService, Events, ProductCreatedEvent } from '../types.js';\nimport { v4 as uuid } from 'uuid';\n\ninterface Product {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  price: number;\n  category: string;\n}\n\nexport function createCatalogService(bus: EventBus): CatalogService {\n  const products = new Map<string, Product>();\n\n  return {\n    createProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string {\n      const id = uuid();\n      products.set(id, { id, name, price, category });\n      bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, { productId: id, name, price, category } satisfies ProductCreatedEvent);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getProduct(productId: string) {\n      return products.get(productId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    listProducts(category?: string) {\n      const all = Array.from(products.values());\n      if (category) {\n        return all.filter(p => p.category === category);\n      }\n      return all;\n    },\n\n    searchProducts(query: string) {\n      const lower = query.toLowerCase();\n      return Array.from(products.values()).filter(\n        p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(lower) || p.category.toLowerCase().includes(lower)\n      );\n    },\n\n    updateProduct(productId: string, updates: Partial<{ name: string; price: number; category: string }>) {\n      const existing = products.get(productId);\n      if (!existing) throw new Error(`Product ${productId} not found`);\n      products.set(productId, { ...existing, ...updates });\n    },\n\n    removeProduct(productId: string): boolean {\n      return products.delete(productId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about whether `updateProduct` and `updateStatus` should throw or just silently do nothing. Looking at the interfaces:\n- `updateProduct` doesn't return anything (void)\n- `removeProduct` returns boolean\n- `updateStatus` returns void\n\nFor updateProduct, if the product doesn't exist, throwing makes sense since it's a void return. For updateStatus on Orders, if an invalid transition, also throw.\n\nLet me flesh out each service now.\n\nLet me write all files efficiently.\n\nFor payment, looking at the interface again:\n```typescript\nexport interface PaymentService {\n  processPayment(orderId: string, amount: number): Promise<{ status: PaymentStatus; transactionId?: string; error?: string }>;\n  refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n}\n```\n\nAnd it says \"The payment service additionally accepts an optional options parameter for simulation configuration.\"\n\nSo the factory is: `createPaymentService(bus: EventBus, options?: PaymentOptions)`\n\n```typescript\ninterface PaymentOptions {\n  mode?: 'success' | 'failure' | 'timeout';\n  delayMs?: number;\n}\n```\n\nOK, now let me write all services. I'll be very careful about types and events.\n\nFor the PaymentService, when it processes a payment:\n- It should emit PAYMENT_PROCESSED with the result\n- It should wait for the delay (async)\n- Based on mode:\n  - success: generate transactionId, emit with status SUCCESS\n  - failure: emit with status FAILED and error message\n  - timeout: emit with status TIMEOUT and error message\n\nFor refund:\n- transactionId must exist\n- Return true if successful, false if not\n\nLet me also think about what events each service should listen to:\n\n1. **Pricing** listens for: PRODUCT_CREATED (to track product prices)\n2. **Notifications** listens for: ORDER_CREATED, ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, PAYMENT_PROCESSED\n3. **Analytics** listens for: everything\n\nServices should register their event handlers in the factory function.