hronous - it just does a Map.set. So when `bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, payload)` is called (not awaited):\n1. Bus pushes to log synchronously\n2. Gets handlers\n3. Calls `await handler(payload)` - first yield point\n4. The handler returns immediately (sync), but await causes a microtask\n5. bus.emit returns a pending Promise\n6. The microtask runs and calls the handler\n\nBut the test then calls `orders.updateStatus(id, OrderStatus.CONFIRMED)` which also calls `bus.emit`. This pushes more events.\n\nThen `getNotifications('user1')` is called. At this point, the microtask queue might have processed the ORDER_CREATED handler, or it might not have.\n\nActually, I think the behavior depends on the JavaScript engine's treatment of async functions. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nIn V8:\n- `async function emit() { ... await handler(payload); ... }` \n- When you call this function (without await), it starts executing synchronously\n- The first `await handler(payload)` is where it actually suspends\n- `handler(payload)` returns a value (not a Promise, since it's sync)\n- `await` wraps this in `Promise.resolve(value)` and then yields\n\nSo steps:\n1. `bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, ...)` starts executing\n2. Event log push (sync)\n3. Gets handlers\n4. Calls `await handler(payload)` - handler is sync, so handler runs immediately\n5. The await wraps the return value and puts the resumption on the microtask queue\n6. bus.emit returns a Promise (which is ignored since not awaited)\n7. Execution continues in the calling function\n\nWait, but step 4 - does `await handler(payload)` execute the handler synchronously? YES! Let me reconsider.\n\nFrom MDN: \"When an async function is called, it returns a Promise. When the async function returns a value, the Promise will be resolved with the returned value. When the async function throws an exception, the Promise will be rejected with the thrown value.\"\n\nAnd: \"await expression causes async function execution to pause until a Promise is settled.\"\n\nSo `await handler(payload)`:\n1. handler(payload) is called synchronously\n2. If handler returns a non-Promise value, await wraps it in Promise.resolve()\n3. The function's execution is paused after this expression\n4. The rest of the function continues on a microtask\n\nSo actually, `handler(payload)` DOES execute synchronously! The handler runs, sets the map entry, and THEN execution pauses. After all handlers have been called (each awoken via microtask), the emit function continues.\n\nWait no, let me re-read: \"await expression causes async function execution to pause until a Promise is settled.\" This means the handler is called, but its result is awaited - control is yielded to the microtask queue.\n\nBut the handler itself ran synchronously (it's a sync function). So the map.set happened. But `bus.emit`'s execution of the next handler (for the next handler in the for loop) is deferred.\n\nLet me trace more carefully:\n\n```javascript\nasync emit(event, payload) {\n  this.eventLog.push({ event, payload, timestamp: Date.now() });\n  const handlers = this.handlers.get(event);\n  if (handlers) {\n    for (const handler of handlers) {\n      await handler(payload);  // <-- THIS is the line\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen `emit` is called without await (like `bus.emit('event', data)`):\n\n1. The async function starts executing synchronously\n2. `this.eventLog.push(...)` - runs synchronously\n3. `this.handlers.get(event)` - runs synchronously\n4. Enters the for loop\n5. `await handler(payload)`:\n   a. `handler(payload)` is called synchronously - handler runs to completion\n   b. `await` receives the return value (which is `undefined` for a sync void function, or a Promise if async)\n   c. If it's a non-Promise value, `await` converts it to `Promise.resolve(undefined)`\n   d. The async function yields here, returning a Promise\n   e. The rest of the loop iteration is enqueued as a microtask\n\nSo the handler IS called synchronously! The map.set in NotificationService happens synchronously. The map.set in AnalyticsService also happens synchronously.\n\nSo why don't the analytics tests work?\n\nLet me look at the analytics test more carefully:\n\n```typescript\nit('returns orders / unique carts', () => {\n  cart.createCart('user1'); // emits cart:updated\n  const cartId2 = cart.createCart('user2');\n  orders.createOrder('user2', cartId2, 50);\n\n  expect(analytics.getConversionRate()).toBe(0.5);\n});\n```\n\nIn the analytics service:\n```typescript\nbus.on(Events.CART_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n  const event = payload as CartUpdatedEvent;\n  uniqueCarts.add(event.cartId);\n  cartItemsByCartId.set(event.cartId, event.items);\n});\n```\n\nThis handler should run synchronously when `bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, ...)` is called (even though emit is async and not awaited).\n\nHmm wait, `orderedId` and `orderCount` - let me check:\n\n```typescript\nbus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n  const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n  orderCount++;\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nThese should also run synchronously.\n\nLet me debug this by checking what the actual values are. Maybe the issue is that `createCart` doesn't actually emit `cart:updated`, or the analytics handler doesn't fire.\n\nActually, I just realized - let me check the `createCart` implementation:\n\n```typescript\ncreateCart(userId: string): string {\n  const id = uuid();\n  carts.set(id, { id, userId, items: new Map() });\n  emitCartUpdate(carts.get(id)!);\n  return id;\n}\n```\n\nAnd `emitCartUpdate`:\n```typescript\nfunction emitCartUpdate(cart: Cart): void {\n  const items = Array.from(cart.items.entries()).map(([productId, quantity]) => ({ productId, quantity }));\n  const payload: CartUpdatedEvent = { cartId: cart.id, userId: cart.userId, items };\n  bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, payload);\n}\n```\n\nSo `createCart` calls `emitCartUpdate` which calls `bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, payload)` without await.\n\nThe emit is 